{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788199639881,"sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_12fee48f04d54d138d138686","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n\ndef load(fn):\n    with open('data/kaggle/'+fn, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nbra = load('Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nnovo = load('novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nbrf = load('BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\n\n# overlap: Brasileirao vs novo by (season, home-ish, away-ish)\ndef key_bra(r): return (r['season'], r['home_team'].split('-')[0].lower(), r['away_team'].split('-')[0].lower())\ndef key_novo(r): return (r['Ano'], r['Equipe_mandante'].split('-')[0].lower(), r['Equipe_visitante'].split('-')[0].lower())\n\nkb = set(map(key_bra, bra)); kn = set(map(key_novo, novo))\nprint('bra keys:', len(kb), 'novo keys:', len(kn), 'overlap:', len(kb & kn))\n\n# check score agreement on overlapping matches\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nbra_by = defaultdict(list)\nfor r in bra: bra_by[key_bra(r)].append(r)\nagree = disagree = 0\nfor r in novo:\n    k = key_novo(r)\n    if k in bra_by:\n        for b in bra_by[k]:\n            if b['home_goal'] == r['Gols_mandante'] and b['away_goal'] == r['Gols_visitante']: agree += 1\n            else: disagree += 1\nprint('score agree:', agree, 'disagree:', disagree)\nEOF"},"output":"bra keys: 3629 novo keys: 6737 overlap: 1160\nscore agree: 1160 disagree: 0\n","metadata":{"output":"bra keys: 3629 novo keys: 6737 overlap: 1160\nscore agree: 1160 disagree: 0\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n\ndef load(fn):\n    with open('data/kaggle/'+fn, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nbra = load('Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nnovo = load('novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nbrf = load('BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\n\n# overlap: Brasileirao vs novo by (season, home-ish, away-ish)\ndef key_bra(r): return (r['season'], r['home_team'].split('-')[0].lower(), r['away_team'].split('-')[0].lower())\ndef key_novo(r): return (r['Ano'], r['Equipe_mandante'].split('-')[0].lower(), r['Equipe_visitante'].split('-')[0].lower())\n\nkb = set(map(key_bra, bra)); kn = set(map(key_novo, novo))\nprint('bra keys:', len(kb), 'novo keys:', len(kn), 'overlap:', len(kb & kn))\n\n# check score agreement on overlapping matches\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nbra_by = defaultdict(list)\nfor r in bra: bra_by[key_bra(r)].append(r)\nagree = disagree = 0\nfor r in novo:\n    k = key_novo(r)\n    if k in bra_by:\n        for b in bra_by[k]:\n            if b['home_goal'] == r['Gols_mandante'] and b['away_goal'] == r['Gols_visitante']: agree += 1\n            else: disagree += 1\nprint('score agree:', agree, 'disagree:', disagree)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788199639874,"end":1788199639880}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a clear picture of the data. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n**Architecture:**\n\n```\n├── server.py                 # MCP server entry point (MCPServer from mcp 2.x)\n├── soccer_mcp/\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── models.py             # dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamStats\n│   ├── loaders.py            # CSV loaders for all 6 files (date parsing, normalization)\n│   ├── normalize.py          # team name normalization (accents, suffixes, aliases)\n│   ├── analytics.py          # stats: standings, head-to-head, team records, biggest wins, averages\n│   └── service.py            # high-level query service combining everything\n├── tests/\n│   ├── test_loaders.py\n│   ├── test_normalize.py\n│   ├── test_analytics.py\n│   ├── test_service.py  (BDD-style scenarios)\n│   └── test_server.py    (tool registration + call_tool smoke tests)\n├── requirements.txt\n└── pytest.ini / pyproject.toml\n```\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n\n1. **Team name normalization** — the core challenge. Normalize:\n   - Strip state suffixes: \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"Palmeiras\"; \"América - MG\" → \"América\"; \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" (strip trailing \" - XX\" where XX is a state)\n   - Remove accents (NFKD → ASCII) for matching: \"Grêmio\" → \"Gremio\", \"São Paulo\" → \"Sao Paulo\"\n   - Case-fold\n   - Common aliases: \"Atlético-MG\" vs \"Atlético Mineiro\" (FIFA), \"Athletico-PR\"/\"Atlético-PR\" vs \"Atlético Paranaense\" (FIFA), \"Sport\" vs \"Sport Club do Recife\", \"América-MG\" vs \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\", \"Ceará\" vs \"Ceará Sporting Club\", \"Vasco da Gama\" vs \"Vasco\", \"Red Bull Bragantino\" vs \"Bragantino\"? etc.\n\n   Approach: normalize_team(name) → canonical key. Build alias map for known variants. For lookup, accept any name, normalize it, and match against normalized team keys. Also do fuzzy substring matching for unknown teams.\n\n   Let me design the canonical key: lowercase, strip accents, remove state suffix, strip punctuation/spaces. E.g.:\n   - \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\"\n   - \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\"\n   - \"América - MG\" → \"america\" — hmm, but there's also \"America-RN\". Ambiguity! \"América-MG\" and \"América-RN\" both normalize to \"america\". \n\n   Hmm, this is a real issue. In novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv there are both \"América-MG\" and \"América-RN\". In Brasileirao_Matches.csv: \"America-MG\". In FIFA: \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\".\n\n   Solution: keep the state as part of disambiguation when available, but the canonical display name should be a \"nice\" name. For matching, I'll use a two-level approach:\n   - Primary key: normalized name without state (e.g. \"america\")\n   - If ambiguous, keep state suffix in the key when the dataset distinguishes them: e.g. \"america-mg\" vs \"america-rn\".\n   \n   Actually a cleaner approach: normalize to name WITHOUT state for display (\"América (MG)\" for display), but internally treat teams from datasets as distinct entities keyed by (normalized_name, state) when both present. When a user queries \"América\", return both/ambiguity info.\n\n   Let me simplify: canonical key = normalized base name + \"-\" + state if state known and base is ambiguous across dataset; otherwise base name. Hmm, this gets complex.\n\n   Alternative pragmatic approach used by most such demos:\n   - canonical_key = strip accents, lowercase, remove state suffix, collapse whitespace/punctuation.\n   - For disambiguation, teams like América-MG and América-RN get separate keys \"americamg\"/\"americarn\" — no wait.\n\n   Let me look at actual overlaps. The teams that appear with state suffix and could collide:\n   - América-MG, América-RN (both in novo)\n   - Athletico-PR vs Atlético-PR (same club, different spellings across eras!)\n   - Atletico-GO vs Atlético-GO\n   \n   In novo file: \"Atlético-GO\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"Athletico-PR\", \"América-MG\", \"América-RN\". Note novo has both \"Atlético-MG\" and... let me check. It has 'Atlético-GO', 'Atlético-MG', 'Athletico-PR' — so within novo, \"Atlético-MG\" appears once (no collision with Atlético-PR since Athletico spelling differs).\n   \n   Actually collisions *within* the same normalized key that are actually DIFFERENT clubs:\n   - america (MG) vs america (RN) — different clubs\n   - atletico: \"Atletico-GO\", \"Atletico-MG\" (Brasileirao file, unaccented) — after stripping state both are \"atletico\" — different clubs!\n   \n   So stripping state blindly merges Atlético-MG and Atlético-GO into \"atletico\". That's wrong.\n\n   Better: canonical key = normalized(name-with-state-suffix-intact-if-the-name-itself-contains-it)? No...\n\n   Cleanest approach: keep a registry mapping normalized full forms (with state) AND normalized base forms:\n   - Each team entity has: display name, state (optional), aliases.\n   - Match priority: exact normalized match with state → exact normalized match without state (if unique) → alias map → fuzzy substring.\n\n   For query resolution: given user input \"Palmeiras\", normalize → \"palmeiras\". Look up in alias→entity map. \"atletico-mg\" → entity Atlético-MG. \"atletico\" alone → ambiguous (GO, MG, PR) → resolve via interactive disambiguation? For a server, better to return all matches or pick the one with most matches in dataset, noting ambiguity.\n\n   Hmm, but wait — for head-to-head and stats queries, ambiguity should be surfaced. Let me design:\n\n   `resolve_team(query) -> list[Team]` — returns matching teams (usually 1). Tools handle multiple matches by reporting them.\n\n   For simplicity and robustness:\n   - Team entity registry built from all match files + FIFA clubs (Brazilian ones only? no, all clubs but only Brazilian clubs are useful for matching to match data).\n   - Each entity has canonical display name like \"Palmeiras\" (no state), state \"SP\" if known.\n   - Key: normalized base name; if multiple distinct states share the same base name, key includes state: \"america-mg\", \"america-rn\", \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico-go\", \"atletico-pr\".\n   \n   So the rule: base name + state if the base is ambiguous; else base name. When user queries \"america\", we find keys starting with \"america\" → both; we can return both or the most prominent. I'll surface ambiguity in results but default to all matches (stats aggregated across both would be wrong; better to report both teams separately).\n\n   Hmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe simpler to always key by base-name+state when state is known, and when resolving a query, try:\n   1. normalized query with state (if user typed \"atletico-mg\")\n   2. base-name key (if exists, e.g., \"palmeiras\")\n   3. base-name prefix matches (all keys starting with base) → if exactly 1, use it; if multiple, return all.\n   \n   And alias map handles FIFA club names: \"Atlético Mineiro\" → atletico-mg, \"Atlético Paranaense\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\" → atletico-pr, \"Sport Club do Recife\" → sport-pe, \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → america-mg, \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → ceara-ce, \"Vasco da Gama\" → vasco, \"Red Bull Bragantino\" → bragantino...\n\n   Wait, in Brasileirao_Matches.csv it's \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\". In novo? Not present (novo covers 2003-2019; RB Bragantino promoted 2019 — let me check). Alias: \"bragantino\" base name.\n\n   Also \"Coritiba\" vs \"Coritiba-PR\" etc. The base-name approach handles those.\n\n   Also Libertadores international teams: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Boca Juniors\", \"River Plate\" etc. These have country codes in parens or suffixes like \"-EQU\". For normalization: strip \"(URU)\" country parens? For display keep them. Base name \"nacional\" would be ambiguous with \"Nacional-AM\"? Actually in Copa do Brasil there might be \"Nacional - AM\" etc. Let me check... There are many small clubs in Copa do Brasil. The base-name disambiguation rule handles it: keys like \"nacional\" vs \"nacional-uru\"? Hmm, for international teams I'd keep the country in the key to disambiguate: \"nacional (URU)\" normalized \"nacionaluru\"... \n\n   Let me keep it pragmatic: \n   - normalize_key(name): NFKD→ascii, lowercase, remove content in parens, strip trailing \" - XX\" or \"-XX\" state suffix IF XX is a 2-letter Brazilian state code, collapse non-alphanumerics.\n   - Wait but \"(URU)\" removal would make \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional\" and Copa do Brasil's \"Nacional - AM\" (if exists) → \"nacional\". Different teams, same key. Then key collision → they'd merge. Bad but... let me check if \"Nacional\" appears in both.\n\n   Actually, better to NOT remove parens content in the key, only state suffixes: \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacionaluru\" — hmm that makes substring matching harder but exact matching fine.\n\n   Alternative: I'll use a smarter approach — the key includes the state/country discriminator ONLY when needed for uniqueness (computed after loading all teams). Plan:\n   1. Load all team names from all sources.\n   2. Compute base_key for each (strip state suffix, strip parens, strip accents, lowercase).\n   3. Group by base_key. For groups with a single entity → key = base_key. For groups with multiple entities → key = base_key + \"-\" + discriminator (state code or country), and ALSO register prefix matching.\n\n   For query resolution:\n   1. Exact key match on normalized full input (with suffix) — try \"atleticomg\".\n   2. Exact base key match — \"palmeiras\".\n   3. If no exact, find keys that start with the query base → all matches.\n   4. Alias map (FIFA full club names → keys).\n   5. Substring matching fallback.\n\n   Entities: also unify same club across datasets. E.g. Brasileirao's \"Atletico-MG\" and novo's \"Atlético-MG\" — same base_key \"atleticomg\"... wait with state suffix kept: \"atleticomg\" both (after accent strip + suffix strip + collapse). But is \"Atletico-MG\" ambiguous with \"Atletico-GO\"? base \"atletico\" appears with MG, GO, PR states → ambiguous group → keys \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico-go\", \"atletico-pr\". But wait, suffix stripping happens BEFORE grouping: \"Atlético-MG\" → base \"atletico\". Then group \"atletico\" has 3 entities → each keeps state → key \"atletico-mg\" etc. And user query \"atletico-mg\" → normalized \"atleticomg\" → hmm, key format matters. Let me use \"atletico-mg\" style keys with separator for readability. Query normalization: \"Atlético-MG\" → lowercase ascii \"atletico-mg\" → exact key match. \n\n   For FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" → no state suffix → base \"atletico mineiro\" → alias map: {\"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico paranaense\": \"atletico-pr\", ...}. I'll build a hand-curated alias table for the ~25 major Brazilian clubs, since data quality demands it (\"Handles team name variations correctly\" is a success criterion).\n\n   Actually — hold on. Let me reconsider. Rather than agonize, let me check what team name forms actually exist across ALL match files and FIFA Brazilian clubs, then design the alias table empirically. Let me dump the distinct team names.\n\n2. **Date parsing**: \n   - \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" (datetime with time)\n   - \"29/03/2003\" (Brazilian DD/MM/YYYY)\n   - \"2023-09-24\" (ISO date)\n   - Possibly empty/invalid → None.\n   Use dateutil? Not installed. Write my own parser: try formats [\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\"]. That's sufficient.\n\n3. **Goals parsing**: Libertadores has goals as strings like \"2\" possibly with quotes — and maybe empty? Check for empty/invalid. BR-Football has floats \"1.0\". Parse int safely.\n\n4. **Competitions**: I'll tag each match with a competition: \"Brasileirão Série A\" (from Brasileirao_Matches + novo historical), \"Copa do Brasil\" (Brazilian_Cup + BR-Football's Copa do Brasil), \"Copa Libertadores\", \"Série B\", \"Série C\" (BR-Football), etc. Should I merge Brasileirao_Matches with novo_campeonato_brasileiro? They overlap (2012-2019 both have Serie A). Hmm — novo is 2003-2019, Brasileirao_Matches is 2012-2022. Overlapping 2012-2019 would duplicate matches. \n\n   Wait, actually are they the same matches? novo has 6886 matches over 17 seasons ≈ 405/season. Brasileirao_Matches has 4180 over 11 seasons = 380/season. Both are Serie A. If I merge, 2012-2019 matches appear twice.\n\n   Options: (a) keep as separate sources, mark source file; for standings use dedup; (b) treat them as distinct datasets and let tools accept a source parameter; (c) dedupe by (date, home, away) when computing standings.\n\n   For standings queries (\"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\"), computing from duplicated data would double points. So: for standings, dedupe matches by (date, home_key, away_key). For general match searches, keep both but mark source... Actually duplicated match listings would look weird for \"Show me all Flamengo vs Fluminense matches\". Hmm.\n\n   Actually wait — let me check if they really overlap. Let me compare: novo 2019 Flamengo matches vs Brasileirao_Matches 2019 Flamengo matches. Let me verify empirically. If they overlap, I'll dedupe on load into a unified match store keyed by (date, home, away, score) — but keep provenance. Hmm, but the task says \"All 6 CSV files are loadable and queryable\" — files must be loadable (they are, into the unified store with provenance). Deduplication at query time for standings is the important correctness fix.\n\n   Let me check the overlap first, then decide. Simple approach: maintain `matches` list where each Match has `source` field. Provide `dedupe=True` logic in standings computation. For match search, group identical (date, home, away) from multiple sources, showing once with sources listed. Actually — simplest correct approach: dedupe at load time into canonical match records, with `sources: list[str]` field. Each CSV row still \"loaded and queryable\" — the data is there. I think dedupe-at-load with multi-source provenance is cleanest and gives correct standings automatically.\n\n   But careful: BR-Football-Dataset \"Serie A\" ALSO overlaps with Brasileirao_Matches (2023 dates, e.g. 2023-09-24 Sao Paulo vs Flamengo... wait, Brasileirao_Matches only goes to 2022. BR-Football covers 2014-2023 including Serie A. So BR-Football Serie A 2014-2022 overlaps Brasileirao_Matches too!). Ugh. And BR-Football team names are unaccented (\"Sao Paulo\", \"Gremio\"?). Let me check.\n\n   OK here's the plan: unified Match store, dedupe by (date, home_key, away_key) — when scores agree, merge sources; when scores differ (data conflict), keep both? No — keep the first (priority: Brasileirao_Matches > novo > BR-Football?) hmm. Let me check empirically how much they overlap and whether scores agree.\n\n   Wait, actually — maybe BR-Football \"Serie A\" 2014-2022 vs Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2022: BR-Football has 3689 Serie A matches. Brasileirao has 4180 (2012-2022, 11 seasons). BR-Football Serie A probably covers 2014-2023. Overlap 2014-2022 = 9 seasons.\n\n   Let me just check empirically. If they overlap with same scores, dedupe is safe and standings correct.\n\n5. **Standings**: compute from Serie A matches per season: 3 pts win, 1 draw. Sort by points, then wins, then goal difference, then goals for. Champion = first. Relegated = bottom 4. This must handle double round-robin; matches with missing scores should be excluded from standings but noted.\n\n6. **Tools** (MCP tools) — map to required capabilities:\n   - `search_matches(team1=None, team2=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=...)` — match queries\n   - `head_to_head(team1, team2, competition=None)` — h2h records\n   - `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\"|\"home\"|\"away\")` — W/D/L, goals\n   - `team_profile(team)` — matches played, competitions played in, seasons, FIFA players at club (cross-file!)\n   - `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=...)` — player queries\n   - `top_players(club=None, nationality=None, limit=10)` — highest rated\n   - `standings(competition=\"brasileirao\", season=2019)` — full table, champion, relegated\n   - `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` \n   - `competition_stats(competition, season=None)` — avg goals/match, home win rate, etc.\n   - `derbies(season=None)` — matches between classic rival pairs (Fla-Flu, Paulista derby, etc.)\n   - `answer(question=...)`? No — that's the LLM's job. The MCP server provides tools; the LLM composes. But maybe a general `ask` fallback isn't needed. The spec says \"MCP server connected to an LLM for query processing\" — the LLM uses the tools. Good.\n   - `list_teams(competition=None, season=None)` — browse teams\n   - `list_competitions()` — available competitions/seasons coverage\n\n   Tool count is fine.\n\n7. **Performance**: load CSVs once at startup (module-level lazy singleton). 18k players + 24k matches load in <1s. All queries in-memory → fast.\n\n8. **Tests**: pytest BDD-style. Test data loading, normalization, match search, h2h, standings, players, stats, and MCP server tool registration + a client call. For server tests, use `mcp.client` with in-memory session? mcp 2.x — check client API. There's `mcp.ClientSession`? Let me check. In mcp 1.x it was `mcp.ClientSession` + `mcp.client.stdio_client`. In 2.x probably similar. I can test tool listing/calling in-memory via `server.call_tool()` directly — simplest: instantiate MCPServer, call `await server.list_tools()` and `await server.call_tool(...)`. Let me check call_tool signature.\n\nLet me now check the data overlaps and team name forms.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059011ad00011AljhZ7l02kiZQ","sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","messageID":"msg_058ffb560001aWP8WEj28d4pNQ"}}
