  tools = await s.list_tools()\n   result = await s.call_tool(\"search_matches\", {...})\n   ```\n   Check list_tools/call_tool signatures. Let me inspect.\n\n   Also there's the client: `mcp.Client`? For an end-to-end test over stdio would spawn subprocess — heavy. Direct call_tool is fine + maybe an in-memory client session test. Let me check what client APIs exist in mcp 2.x.\n\n   Output format: tools return nicely formatted markdown-ish text (like the examples in the spec). Spec shows text formats like \"Flamengo vs Fluminense (Fla-Flu derby): ...\" — I'll produce those.\n\n   Let me now check a few more data details, then write the code.\n\n   Files to write:\n   - `soccer_mcp/__init__.py`\n   - `soccer_mcp/normalize.py` — team name normalization & aliasing\n   - `soccer_mcp/models.py` — dataclasses Match, Player, TeamRecord, etc.\n   - `soccer_mcp/loaders.py` — CSV parsing for the 6 files → unified stores\n   - `soccer_mcp/service.py` — SoccerDataService: resolution + all query methods\n   - `soccer_mcp/analytics.py` — standings, h2h, stats computations (maybe merge into service; better separate for clarity)\n   - `server.py` — MCP server wiring with tools\n   - `tests/conftest.py` — session-scoped service fixture\n   - `tests/test_normalize.py`\n   - `tests/test_loaders.py`\n   - `tests/test_queries.py` — BDD scenarios (Given/When/Then style with docstrings)\n   - `tests/test_analytics.py` — standings, h2h, biggest wins\n   - `tests/test_players.py`\n   - `tests/test_server.py` — MCP tool registration + calls\n   - `requirements.txt`\n   - `pyproject.toml` or `pytest.ini` — pytest config\n   - Update README? The task says implement TASK.md; README update is nice-to-have. I'll add usage instructions to README.\n\n   Performance requirement: load once (module singleton with lazy init). ~25k matches + 18k players. CSV parse ~1-2s. Fine.\n\n   Let me check some more data quirks quickly:\n   1. BR-Football vs Brasileirao score agreement\n   2. FIFA empty values /