{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788199937520,"sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_1a8503d7a42f4735a33695e7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint(\"call_tool:\", inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint()\nprint(inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.call_tool)[:1500])\nEOF"},"output":"call_tool: (self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\n\nCall a tool by name with arguments.\n\nRaises:\n    ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n        tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n    UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n        its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n        exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n    MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n","metadata":{"output":"call_tool: (self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\n\nCall a tool by name with arguments.\n\nRaises:\n    ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n        tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n    UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n        its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n        exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n    MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint(\"call_tool:\", inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint()\nprint(inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.call_tool)[:1500])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788199937485,"end":1788199937519}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the patterns. Brazilian state suffixes use 2-letter codes; international country suffixes use 3-letter codes or parentheses: \"-EQU\", \"(PAR)\", \"-PAR\", \"(URU)\", \"-URU\", \"(VEN)\", \"(PER)\", \"-PER\".\n\nTime to design and write the implementation. Let me now plan the module structure concretely:\n\n```\nserver.py                        # MCP server entry point\nsoccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  models.py                      # Match, Player dataclasses\n  normalize.py                   # accent stripping, team name parsing, alias table, TeamRegistry\n  loaders.py                     # load all 6 CSVs into unified stores (with dedup)\n  analytics.py                   # standings, team stats, h2h, biggest wins, averages, derbies\n  service.py                     # SoccerDataService facade with all query methods\n  formatting.py?                 # maybe inline in service\ntests/\n  conftest.py\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_loaders.py\n  test_match_queries.py\n  test_team_queries.py\n  test_player_queries.py\n  test_competition_queries.py\n  test_statistics.py\n  test_server.py\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml (pytest config)\n```\n\nLet me define the details:\n\n**normalize.py**:\n- `strip_accents(s)` — NFKD, remove combining, case handled separately.\n- `BRAZILIAN_STATES` — 27 UFs.\n- `COUNTRY_CODES` — {URU, PAR, EQU, PER, VEN, ARG, CHI, COL, BOL, MEX, ECU→EQU...} use the ones seen: EQU, PAR, URU, VEN, PER + common others (ARG, CHI, COL, BOL, MEX).\n- `parse_team_name(raw) -> (base, region)`:\n  1. strip whitespace\n  2. remove parenthesized country: `(URU)` → region, remove from name\n  3. trailing suffix patterns: ` - XX`, `-XX`, ` XX` where XX is 2-letter UF → region; or 3-letter country → region\n  4. base = strip accents, lowercase, remove punctuation (`.` too, e.g. \"A.b.c.\" hmm \"A.b.c. - RN\" — lowercase abc... base \"a b c\"? Let me remove dots → \"abc\"). Collapse whitespace.\n- `ALIASES` dict (normalized alias → (base, region)):\n  ```\n  atletico mineiro → (atletico, MG)\n  atletico paranaense → (atletico, PR)\n  athletico paranaense → (atletico, PR)\n  athletico → (atletico, PR)\n  atletico goianiense → (atletico, GO)\n  atletico goianiense? \"Atletico Goianiense\" — BR-Football. yes\n  ca parana → (parana, PR)\n  sport recife → (sport, PE)\n  sport club do recife → (sport, PE)\n  vasco da gama → (vasco, RJ)\n  america fc natal → (america, RN)\n  america fc minas gerais → (america, MG)   # from FIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — parens removed by parser? \"(Minas Gerais)\" is not a country code... parser removes parens content only if it's a country code. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → parens content \"minas gerais\" not a code → keep in name? Better: normalized alias \"america fc minas gerais\" → map to (america, MG).\n  vitoria ec → (vitoria, BA)? wait \"Vitoria EC\" appears in BR-Football = Esporte Clube Vitória (BA). \"EC Vitoria\" too. \"Vitória F. C.\" hmm — \"Vitoria F. C. - ES\" is Vitória FC (ES)? Hmm, actually there might be \"Rio Branco-Vitória\"... \"Vitoria F. C. - ES\" — hmm. I'll map vitoria ec/ec vitoria → (vitoria, BA), vitoria fc → (vitoria, BA)? But \"Vitoria F. C. - ES\" normalized \"vitoria fc\" with state ES → (vitoria, ES) by parsing. Conflict: alias applied before or after state parse? Apply parse first (gets ES), alias only for exact full-name matches. \"Vitoria F. C. - ES\" parses to base \"vitoria f c\" + ES → distinct from \"vitoria\"+BA. OK good, no alias needed for that one. But \"Vitoria EC\"/\"EC Vitoria\" (BR-Football, stateless) → base \"vitoria ec\"/\"ec vitoria\" → alias → (vitoria, BA).\n  ec bahia → (bahia, BA)\n  fortaleza ec → (fortaleza, CE); fortaleza fc → (fortaleza, CE)? \"Fortaleza FC\" — hmm could be Fortaleza-CE. Yes BR-Football. But also maybe \"Fortaleza FC\" from elsewhere... fine.\n  macae esporte fc / macae esporte rj → (macae, RJ)\n  portuguesa desportos → (portuguesa, SP)\n  nautico capibaribe → (nautico, PE)\n  gremio barueri → (barueri, SP); gremio prudente → (barueri, SP); barueri → (barueri, SP)\n  red bull bragantino → (bragantino, SP)\n  bragantino → hmm stateless \"Bragantino - PA\" has state PA. Is there stateless \"Bragantino\"? From the dump: 'Bragantino', 'Bragantino - PA', 'Bragantino - SP', 'Bragantino PA'. Stateless 'Bragantino' exists — from where? Probably BR-Football Serie B older seasons (CA Bragantino before Red Bull, 2014-2019 Serie B) = the SP club. So stateless \"bragantino\" → merge with (bragantino, SP) via stateless-merge heuristic (most matches). ✓ automatic.\n  boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira → (boavista, RJ)\n  operario ferroc... \"Operario Ferroviario Esporte C - PR\" → (operario, PR) — alias \"operario ferroviario esporte c\" → operario+PR.\n  esportivo bento goncalves → (esportivo, RS)\n  flamengo do piaui → (flamengo, PI); flamengo → stateless → merges with (flamengo, RJ) ✓ auto\n  fluminense de feira → (fluminense de feira, BA) auto by parsing \"Fluminense de Feira - BA\" — base \"fluminense de feira\". And \"Fluminense De Feira\" stateless → base \"fluminense de feira\" ✓ same, stateless-merge with BA ✓.\n  santa cruz fc → (santa cruz, PE)\n  sao paulo → stateless → (sao paulo, SP) ✓ auto\n  santos → stateless → (santos, SP) ✓ auto (vs Santos-AP tiny)\n  ceara sporting club → (ceara, CE)\n  csa → (csa, AL) auto? \"Csa - AL\", \"Csa-AL\", \"CSA\" stateless → merge ✓\n  cuiaba → stateless → (cuiaba, MT) ✓ auto\n  internacional sc?? \"EC Internacional SC\" → base \"ec internacional\" + SC?? hmm \"EC Internacional SC\" — the \"SC\" here is state SC (Santa Catarina) written with space! My parser: trailing \" SC\" 2-letter → region SC, base \"ec internacional\" → alias ec internacional → (internacional, SC)? But \"Internacional - SC\" exists → (internacional, SC). So both → Internacional-SC. But careful: \"Internacional RS\" (BR-Football) → (internacional, RS) ✓. And FIFA \"Internacional\" stateless → merge with RS (most matches) ✓.\n  ```\n  \n  Also from BR-Football: 'America MG' → (america, MG) ✓ parse. 'America RN'? Wait — 'America FC Natal' is América-RN. 'America RN' → parse ✓.\n  \n  Special case: 'Athletico Paranaense - PR' → parse gives base \"athletico paranaense\" + PR → alias → (atletico, PR) ✓. 'Atletico Paranaense' stateless → alias ✓. 'Atlético Paranaense - PR' → ✓.\n\n  Also 'Atletico - PR' (with spaces) → parse \"- PR\" ✓ → (atletico, PR) — wait, but base \"atletico\" + PR — and \"Atletico Mineiro\" alias → (atletico, MG). And \"Atletico - ES\" → (atletico, ES) — a different small club (Atlético-ES? Hmm... there's \"Atlético Esportivo?\" whatever, data says ES). And \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" → (atletico cearense, CE) distinct ✓. \"Real Atlético\" → distinct ✓.\n\n  Region-aware keys: entity key = f\"{base}|{region}\" e.g. \"atletico|MG\", \"palmeiras|SP\", \"flamengo|RJ\", \"botafogo|RJ\", \"botafogo|PB\", \"botafogo|SP\".\n  \n  Stateless-merge: entity (base, None) merges into the (base, R) entity with most matches for that base. If no state variants exist, stays (base, None).\n\n- `TeamRegistry`:\n  - `register(raw_name, count)` — builds entities from all sources.\n  - Actually build from: match files (raw home/away names with match counts), FIFA clubs (players count — for display/cross-ref only).\n  - Entities: key, display_name (nicest raw variant — pick curated map for big clubs else most frequent raw form), region, names (set of raw variants), match_count, player_count.\n  - Display names: curated for big clubs: \"Flamengo\", \"Palmeiras\", \"São Paulo\", \"Corinthians\", \"Santos\", \"Fluminense\", \"Botafogo\", \"Vasco da Gama\", \"Grêmio\", \"Internacional\", \"Cruzeiro\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"Atlético-GO\", \"Athletico-PR\"... hmm display with state where ambiguous: \"Atlético-MG\" vs \"Atlético-GO\" vs \"Athletico-PR\". For clarity display ambiguous ones with region suffix: \"América-MG\", \"América-RN\", \"Botafogo-RJ/PB/SP\"? Hmm \"Botafogo\" alone should display as \"Botafogo\" (the RJ one) — but there are 3 Botafogos in data... For display I'll use: if base name is unique in registry → base display; else base-REGION. But \"Botafogo\" is ambiguous... users asking \"Botafogo\" usually mean RJ. Since entity resolution with prominence ordering handles query disambiguation, display names: curated for the famous ambiguous ones (\"Botafogo\" for RJ, \"América-MG\"/\"América-RN\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"Atlético-GO\", \"Athletico-PR\", \"Santa Cruz-PE\" etc.). I'll have a curated display map and fall back to base-REGION for ambiguous, base for unique.\n\n- `resolve(query) -> list[Team]` sorted by prominence (match_count desc):\n  1. normalize query: strip parens country, accents, lowercase\n  2. exact key match (with region suffix parse) → [entity]\n  3. alias direct\n  4. base exact (unique entity for base) → it\n  5. all entities whose base == query base (multiple → all, prominence sorted)\n  6. substring: entities where query in base or base in query... careful. e.g. \"inter\" → internacional entities. Use base.startswith(query) or query in base.\n  7. also match against raw name variants.\n\n**models.py**:\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None\n    home: Team (or key str + display resolved later)\n    away: ...\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    competition: str  # canonical\n    season: int | None\n    round_: str | None   # round label (\"Round 22\", \"Semifinal\", \"final\")\n    stage: str | None\n    venue: str | None    # arena from novo\n    sources: list[str]\n    # optional stats from BR-Football\n    home_corners/away_corners/home_shots/away_shots/home_attacks/away_attacks: int|None\n    halftime: str|None  # ht_result/at_result \"DRAW/LOST/WON\"?  actually ht_result is from home perspective: WON/DRAW/LOST\n    kickoff: str|None   # time\n```\nKeep teams as keys (strings); service resolves display names.\n\nHmm wait — for matches, I need to store home_team_key/away_team_key (entity keys). Since registry built from same raw names, fine.\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Player:\n    id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, club_key (resolved, may be None), position, position_group, jersey, height, weight, preferred_foot, value, wage, skills: dict[str,int]\n```\n\n**loaders.py**:\n- `parse_date(s)` → date | None — formats: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\"\n- `parse_goals(s)` → int | None — handles \"2\", \"2.0\", \"-\", \"NA\", \"\" → None\n- Load each file → raw match dicts with raw team names, then:\n  1. First pass: registry built from all raw names across files (+ FIFA clubs). Actually two-phase: collect raw names & counts from all match files; build registry; then second pass create Match objects with entity keys.\n  - Implementation: `load_all(data_dir)` → returns registry, matches, players. Phase 1 read all rows; phase 2 build registry; phase 3 map rows to Matches with dedup.\n- Dedup: key = (date, home_key, away_key); if date None use (season, home, away, round?) hmm — the NA Libertadores row has no date/season. Dedup key with None date falls back to (competition, season, home, away, stage). Simpler: dedup key = (competition, date or f\"S{season}\", home_key, away_key, round/stage). Hmm but cross-file dedup needs same competition canonicalization: BR-Football Serie A ↔ Brasileirao_Matches (same competition \"Brasileirão Série A\") ✓; BR-Football Copa do Brasil ↔ Brazilian_Cup_Matches ✓ same canonical. novo ↔ Brasileirao ✓. Dedup key: (competition, season, date, home_key, away_key). When duplicate found: merge sources; fill missing optional fields (stats/venue/time/halftime/round info) if kept row lacks them.\n  - Order: load Brasileirao_Matches, novo, Brazilian_Cup, Libertadores, BR-Football (later = fills stats).\n  - Wait: score conflicts? bra/novo agreed 100%. BR-Football vs bra — unknown, but I keep first-loaded (Bra) score anyway.\n  - What about two-legged same-day? Same date+teams can't happen twice realistically. OK.\n\n**analytics.py**:\n- `team_record(matches, team_key, venue=None)` → W/D/L/GF/GA\n- `head_to_head(matches, key1, key2)` → per-team wins, draws, goals, match list\n- `standings(matches, competition, season)` → list of rows (pts, w/d/l, gf, ga, gd, played) sorted; handles dedup already done at load.\n- `biggest_wins(matches, n)` → by goal margin\n- `competition_stats(matches)` → avg goals, home win%, away win%, draw%, total matches\n- `derby_pairs` — curated: Fla-Flu (Flamengo-Fluminense), Paulista (Corinthians-Palmeiras, Corinthians-São Paulo, Palmeiras-São Paulo, Santos-São Paulo...), Gre-Nal (Grêmio-Internacional), Clássico Vovô (Fluminense-Vasco? actually Vasco-Fluminense? Clássico dos Gigantes... keep it simple), Atlético-MG vs Cruzeiro (Clássico Mineiro), Bahia-Vitória (Ba-Vi), Atlético-GO vs Goiás? (clássico goiano), Sport-Náutico (Clássico dos Clássicos), Ceará-Fortaleza (Clássico-Rei), Athletico-PR vs Coritiba (Paraná derby Atletiba), Avaí-Figueirense (Clássico de Florianópolis), Remo-Paysandu not in data... I'll curate ~15 famous derbies with names.\n\n**service.py** — SoccerDataService with methods returning formatted strings (markdown-ish, matching spec examples):\n- `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=20)` \n- `head_to_head(team1, team2, competition=None)`\n- `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\")`\n- `team_profile(team)` — overview: matches, seasons, competitions, record, trophies (championships computed!), players at club\n- `list_teams(competition=None, season=None)`\n- `list_competitions()` — coverage summary\n- `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)`\n- `top_players(club=None, nationality=None, position=None, limit=10)`\n- `player_profile(name)` — one player detail\n- `standings(competition, season, include_relegation=True)`\n- `champion(competition, season)` — maybe part of standings\n- `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`\n- `stats(competition=None, season=None, team=None)` — aggregates\n- `derbies(season=None, competition=None)`\n- `finals(competition=None, season=None)` — finals matches (Copa do Brasil round 8, Libertadores stage final)\n\nAll return strings formatted like spec examples. Season default None = all. Competition aliases: \"brasileirao\", \"serie a\", \"serie b\", \"serie c\", \"copa do brasil\", \"libertadores\".\n\n**server.py** — MCPServer with @server.tool() wrappers around a module-level service instance (lazy). Tools with good docstrings (LLM-facing). run stdio.\n\nWait — lazy loading: tools are sync functions; service loads data on first use (thread-safe-ish via module global + lock). Load takes ~1-2s; acceptable. Actually precompute at import? For tests it doesn't matter; for server, tool calls after startup. Better: load eagerly in `get_service()` cached.\n\nTest the server tools: instantiate build_server() and call `await server.list_tools()` / `await server.call_tool(name, args)`. Need to check exact call_tool signature in mcp 2.x. Let me inspect. Also check what call_tool returns (CallToolResult with content blocks).\n\nLet me check the API details for call_tool and whether there's a client for in-memory testing.\n\nOne more design point — **the \"20 sample questions\" success criterion**: I'll write a test file that answers 20+ sample questions from the spec through the service/tools, asserting non-empty sensible answers. That directly satisfies the criterion.\n\nAlso performance criterion: simple lookups < 2s, aggregates < 5s — after data loaded (in-memory), queries are ms. Load once at startup. I could add a timing test.\n\nNow — the fifa \"Value\"/\"Wage\" columns like \"€110.5M\" — parse to float millions? For display only. Skip parsing; keep raw strings. Actually player_profile could show them raw. Fine.\n\nHeight/Weight in FIFA are like \"170\" / \"72\" (kg/cm)? FIFA 19: Height in cm as number string, Weight lbs? In this dataset probably cm/kg. Just display raw.\n\nEmpty Position → \"Unknown\"? position_group for '' → None.\n\nAlso — \"Name\" field: \"L. Messi\" style abbreviations. Search by name should be accent/case-insensitive substring; also match full \"Neymar Jr\". Fine.\n\nLet me now check the call_tool API signature and the client API for tests.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05905a3e3001Kpq8bNiu7H8V4Z","sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","messageID":"msg_059046301001YZuhQY4Sb7w1hW"}}
