ts: 2019 (Flamengo), 2003 (Cruzeiro), 2023 (Palmeiras — from BR-Football, is 2023 complete? BR-Football missing-score rows: 0 → yes complete!). Also 2020 Flamengo, 2021 Atlético-MG (2021 Brasileirao file complete? missing only in 2016 & 2022 → yes). 2018 Palmeiras. Great test set: 2003 Cruzeiro, 2018 Palmeiras, 2019 Flamengo, 2020 Flamengo, 2021 Atlético-MG, 2023 Palmeiras.\n\nAlso dedup fill: the merge must fill missing scores from BR-Football duplicates ✓ (30 recovered).\n\nOne more subtlety on dedup keys: (competition, season, home_key, away_key). The 2016 Chapecoense NA match (round 38 vs Atletico-MG, not played after the plane crash — actually it WAS awarded/scoreless; real world: Chapecoense 2016 round 38 wasn't played? The final round after the crash — CBF awarded... whatever). BR-Football Serie A 2016 might have that match with a score? If so dedup fills it. Data-driven either way.\n\nNow — CRITICAL check on my dedup key: (competition, season, home_key, away_key) for Copa do Brasil. Wait — same fixture (home, away) could occur twice in same Copa do Brasil season? Rounds 1-3 single leg; later rounds 2 legs home-and-away. Two legs have swapped venues → different keys ✓. BUT what if a round-1 match AND another round match pair teams same order? No — teams meet at most once per pairing in a cup run. Safe.\n\nBUT WAIT — bigger issue with dedup for Copa do Brasil: BR-Football's Copa do Brasil (1123 matches) vs Brazilian_Cup_Matches (1337). Same competition, overlapping seasons 2014-2021ish. Dedup by (season, home, away) — team name normalization must align them: Brazilian_Cup \"Criciúma - SC\" vs BR-Football \"Criciuma\"; \"Fluminense - RJ\" vs \"Fluminense RJ\"? BR-Football Copa teams: \"Sao Paulo\", \"Flamengo\", \"Gremio\", \"Corinthians\" — stateless! And Brazilian_Cup has \" - RJ\" suffixes. So entity keys: (\"fluminense\", RJ) from Cup file and (\"fluminense\", None) from BR-Football → stateless-merge into (\"fluminense\", RJ) ✓ resolved by registry BEFORE dedup. \n\nBut hmm — BR-Football Copa do Brasil also has small stateless clubs like \"Boavista\" vs Cup's \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\". \"Boavista\" stateless → base \"boavista\" merges with (\"boavista\", RJ) only if stateside exists... the alias maps the long name to (\"boavista\", RJ) ✓ then stateless \"boavista\" merges into it ✓.\n\nWhat about stateless clubs in BR-Football that match MULTIPLE stateside entities, e.g. BR-Football \"Botafogo\" (stateless) with Botafogo RJ/PB/SP existing? BR-Football Serie B has \"Botafogo SP\" (with state) — and stateless \"Botafogo\"? Probably only for RJ. The stateless-merge rule: merge into the most-prominent stateside entity with same base ✓ (RJ).\n\nWhat about BR-Football \"Santos\" (stateless, Serie A) vs (\"santos\", SP) and (\"santos\", AP)? → SP (prominent) ✓.\n\nOK, also \"America\" stateless in BR-Football? They use \"America MG\"/\"America RN\" with states ✓.\n\nAlright — now an important detail for dedup ACROSS files when the same match appears with dates in different files: I'm keying WITHOUT date so it's fine.\n\nBut one more: same (competition, season, home, away) appearing twice WITHIN Brazilian_Cup file (e.g., data error) — dedup keeps first. Fine.\n\nAnd Libertadores: \"Nacional (PAR)\" & \"Nacional-URU\" & \"Nacional (URU)\" → (\"nacional\", PAR) and (\"nacional\", URU) distinct ✓. \"Boca Juniors\" stateless ✓ single entity.\n\nOne more possible collision: Brasileirão has (\"bahia\", BA) and Copa do Brasil has \"Bahia de Feira - BA\" → (\"bahia de feira\", BA) distinct ✓.\n\n\"Internacional - SC\" in Libertadores?? It appeared in the big list from 'Internacional - SC' — which file? Probably Copa do Brasil (Inter de Lages). Distinct from (\"internacional\", RS) ✓ via state.\n\nNow — what about teams from the same state sharing a base? E.g. two different (\"gremio\", RS)?? \"Grêmio Esportivo Sapucaiense - RS\" → (\"gremio esportivo sapucaiense\", RS) distinct base ✓. \"Gremio Anapolis\" → (\"gremio anapolis\", None)... vs (\"gremio\", RS) — distinct ✓.\n\nEdge: \"Grêmio Novorizontino\" → (\"gremio novorizontino\", None) — distinct from Grêmio ✓. And \"Red Bull Bragantino\" alias ✓.\n\nNow — one more possible collision: Brasileirão has (\"bahia\", BA) and Copa do Brasil has \"Bahia de Feira - BA\" → (\"bahia de feira\", BA) distinct ✓.\n\n\"Fortaleza FC\" vs \"Fortaleza EC\" both → (\"fortaleza\", CE) ✓ same club.\n\nNow let me also think about \"Atletico - PR\" — wait, there's an entity conflict: is (\"atletico\", PR) = Athletico Paranaense for ALL years? In novo file \"Athletico-PR\" appears 2019, \"Atlético-PR\" earlier — same club (renamed 2019). Merging is correct.\n\nBUT WAIT — danger: \"Atlético-PR\" in Brasileirao file... and Copa do Brasil has \"Atletico - PR\"... AND \"Athletico Paranaense - PR\". All same ✓.\n\nAnother danger: (\"atletico\", ES) — \"Atletico - ES\" from... earlier dump showed 'Atletico - ES' among names (from Brazilian_Cup file? Let me not worry — it's its own small club, distinct).\n\nHmm, actually — hold on. Which file has \"Atletico - ES\"? From the big variant dump, yes it exists. Distinct entity, fine.\n\nNow let me think about the entity \"prominence\" ordering for stateless merge when a stateless base has ZERO stateside entities — stays its own entity with region None. E.g. \"boca juniors\" (None). ✓\n\nAlso — what if two DIFFERENT stateside entities exist AND a stateless one: stateless merges into most-prominent stateside. If NO stateside but the stateless base appears with 2 different aliases mapping to different regions... e.g. base \"nautico capibaribe\" alias → (\"nautico\", PE) directly at parse — so no issue.\n\nAlright. Now — query resolution \"atletico\" → entities: atletico|MG, atletico|PR, atletico|GO, atletico|ES, atletico|AC? (\"Atlético - AC\" → Atletico Acreano-ish), (\"atletico\", BA)?? \"Atletico Alagoinhas\"→(\"atletico alagoinhas\",None). So resolution returns 5 entities sorted by match count: MG, PR, GO, ... Tools that need ONE team: if multiple, either (a) error with disambiguation list, or (b) pick top + note. For team_stats/h2h I'll REQUIRE resolution to be unambiguous: if multiple matches, return disambiguation message listing options (the LLM can then refine). That's clean MCP design. Hmm — but \"atletico-mg\" typed with suffix → parse → (\"atletico\", MG) → unique ✓. \"atletico mineiro\" → alias ✓ unique. Plain \"atletico\" → ambiguous → disambiguation message. \n\nBut hmm — what about \"sport\"? → (\"sport\", PE) unique? \"Sport Boys\" is base \"sport boys\" distinct. So \"sport\" → sport|PE only ✓. \"bahia\" → (\"bahia\", BA) — also (\"bahia\", None)?? no stateless bahia merges into BA ✓ unique. \"botafogo\" → 3 entities → disambiguation (RJ, PB, SP) with match counts. Good UX: \"Did you mean: Botafogo-RJ (most matches), Botafogo-PB, Botafogo-SP?\". The LLM will refine. But for a question like \"Which team has the best home record?\" (no team param) — different tool (best_home_team in stats).\n\nOK — actually let me reconsider: for team_stats/team_profile with ambiguous input, maybe default to the most prominent with a note? For MCP + LLM usage, disambiguation prompts are BETTER (LLM re-calls with suffix). But it costs a roundtrip. Compromise: `resolve_team(query, strict=True)` returns list; tools: if len==1 use it; if >1 → for stats/profile, pick most prominent AND note \"showing Botafogo-RJ; also found Botafogo-PB, Botafogo-SP\". That answers most user intents immediately (people saying \"botafogo\" mean RJ). I'll do that with a clear note.\n\nHmm, wait — \"america\" → América-MG vs América-RN — most prominent = MG (Serie A). Note included. Fine.\n\nNow — let me also design the **derby map** (for \"Show me all derbies in 2023\"):\n```\nDERBIES = [\n  (\"Fla-Flu\", [\"flamengo|RJ\", \"fluminense|RJ\"]),\n  (\"Clássico dos Milhões\", [\"flamengo|RJ\", \"vasco|RJ\"]),\n  (\"Clássico Vovô\", [\"botafogo|RJ\", \"fluminense|RJ\"]),\n  (\"Clássico da Amizade\", [\"botafogo|RJ\", \"vasco|RJ\"]),\n  (\"Derby Paulista\", [\"corinthians|SP\", \"palmeiras|SP\"]),\n  (\"Majestoso\", [\"corinthians|SP\", \"sao paulo|SP\"]),\n  (\"Choque Rei\", [\"palmeiras|SP\", \"sao paulo|SP\"]),\n  (\"Clássico da Saudade\"? santos-sp: (\"santos|SP\",\"sao paulo|SP\") — \"Clássico dos Campeões\"? I'll call it Santos vs São Paulo),\n  (\"Gre-Nal\", [\"gremio|RS\", \"internacional|RS\"]),\n  (\"Clássico Mineiro\", [\"atletico|MG\", \"cruzeiro|MG\"]),\n  (\"Atletiba\", [\"atletico|PR\", \"coritiba|PR\"]),\n  (\"Ba-Vi\", [\"bahia|BA\", \"vitoria|BA\"]),\n  (\"Clássico-Rei\", [\"ceara|CE\", \"fortaleza|CE\"]),\n  (\"Clássico dos Clássicos\", [\"sport|PE\", \"nautico|PE\"]),\n  (\"Clássico de Florianópolis\"? avai|SC vs figueirense|SC),\n]\n```\nDerby detection: match between the two team keys (either order).\n\n**Player position groups**:\n```\nGK → GK\nCB LCB RCB LB RB LWB RWB → DEF\nCDM LDM RDM CM LCM RCM CAM LAM RAM LM RM → MID\nST CF LS RS LW RW LF RF → FWD\n'' → unknown\n```\n\n**Skill columns** for player profile: show a handful (Pace-related: SprintSpeed/Acceleration, Shooting: Finishing/ShotPower, Passing: ShortPassing/LongPassing/Vision, Dribbling, Defending: Marking/StandingTackle, Physical: Strength/Stamina). For profile show ~6 key attrs.\n\n**Value/Wage parsing**: \"€110.5M\", \"€565K\" → parse to human numbers. Value in M€/K€. Keep raw display.\n\nNow the **tools** on the server. Final list (12 tools):\n1. `list_competitions()` — coverage overview (competitions, seasons, match counts)\n2. `list_teams(competition, season)` — teams in competition/season (by match participation), with match counts; optional FIFA squad size\n3. `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)` — match search incl. H2H-style listing\n4. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition)` — full H2H record + recent matches\n5. `team_stats(team, competition, season, venue)` — record W/D/L GF/GA + win rate\n6. `team_profile(team)` — overview: seasons, competitions, all-time record, titles (from standings), notable players (top-rated at club)\n7. `standings(competition, season)` — full table + champion + relegated (Série A/B; cups → different handling: standings only meaningful for leagues — for cups, report rounds/finals? I'll compute table for any competition but note cups aren't round-robin; better: for cups, tool explains and points to finals/derbies. Actually simplest: standings works for league competitions; for Copa do Brasil/Libertadores return informative message. Hmm — or compute \"general table\" anyway? No — return helpful message.)\n8. `finals(competition, season)` — finals across cups (Copa do Brasil round 8 / Libertadores stage final)\n9. `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, max_overall, limit)` \n10. `player_profile(name)` — detail incl. attributes\n11. `top_players(club, nationality, position, limit)` — highest overall\n12. `biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)` \n13. `stats(competition, season, team)` — aggregate stats: avg goals, home/away/draw rates, biggest win, etc. (when team given → team-focused aggregates?) Keep competition-level + optional season.\n14. `derbies(season, competition)` — derby matches\n\nThat's 14 tools. Good coverage of the spec's 5 categories.\n\nAlso `answer_sample_questions`? No.\n\nTool naming with underscores; parameters snake_case. Return strings (markdown-ish plain text like spec examples).\n\n**Output formats** — follow spec examples closely:\n```\nFlamengo vs Fluminense (Fla-Flu derby):\n- 2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Round 22)\n...\nHead-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins, Fluminense 8 wins, 7 draws\n```\n\nHmm — one nuance: \"Show me all Flamengo vs Fluminense matches\" — spec says search_matches with team+opponent. My search_matches(team=\"Flamengo\", opponent=\"Fluminense\") returns chronological matches + h2h summary. The h2h tool gives fuller record. Fine.\n\n**Standings format**:\n```\n2019 Brasileirão Série A Final Standings (calculated from matches):\n1. Flamengo - 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L) - Champion\n2. Santos - 74 pts ...\n...\nRelegated: ...\n```\nWait — actually 2019: Flamengo 90 pts (26W? let me not hardcode; computed). Real: Flamengo 2019: 26W 12D... hmm 90 pts = 26W+12D? No: 26×3=78+12=90 ✓ (26W 12D 0L?? no, 38 games: 26W 12D 0L impossible... 2019 Flamengo: 26W, 12D, 4L? that's 42 games. Actual: 38 matches, 26W 12D... no. Real 2019: Flamengo W26 D12 L4?? too many. Let me not worry — the computed table is data-driven; test champion only (and maybe points=90 for 2019 — actually real Flamengo 2019: 26 wins, 12 draws... hmm that's 38 games = 26+12+0? No: 26+12+4=42. WRONG. Real: 2019 Flamengo 90 points from 26W 12D 4L?? 38 rounds: 26+12+4 = 42 ≠ 38. So actual: 28W 6D 4L = 84+6=90 ✓ (as spec's example says 28W 6D 4L!). OK the spec example itself says Flamengo 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L). I'll assert champion + points in tests.)\n\n**Série B standings**: also computable (BR-Football Serie B 2014-2023). Real champions for tests: 2021 Série B = Botafogo? Hmm 2021 Série B champion: Botafogo ✓ (they got promoted 2021). Let me verify via data at test-writing time — safer: test only Série A champions (known history) + structural tests (point math, 38 games, sorted order) for others.\n\nWait — 2003-2004 Série A had 46 rounds (24 teams); 2005 42 rounds; 2006+ 38. Structural assertions must be flexible (just check champion + monotonic points).\n\nLet me now also verify known champions against the data DURING test development. Champions by year (real): 2003 Cruzeiro, 2004 Santos, 2005 Corinthians, 2006 São Paulo, 2007 São Paulo, 2008 São Paulo, 2009 Flamengo, 2010 Fluminense, 2011 Corinthians, 2012 Fluminense, 2013 Cruzeiro, 2014 Cruzeiro, 2015 Corinthians, 2016 Palmeiras, 2017 Corinthians, 2018 Palmeiras, 2019 Flamengo, 2020 Flamengo, 2021 Atlético-MG, 2022 Palmeiras, 2023 Palmeiras.\n\nData caveats: 2005 (match-fixing replays — dataset may have both original and replayed matches?? or just one set), 2016 (1 missing score — Chapecoense round 38; champion was Palmeiras anyway), 2022 (51 missing scores — risky), 2011 Corinthians ✓. I'll assert champions for: 2003, 2004, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2023 — skipping 2005 & 2022 (known data gaps). I'll verify empirically when writing tests — if some fail due to data quirks (e.g. 2012 Fluminense...), I'll adjust the tested set and note it.\n\nNow, the biggest remaining unknown: **will my dedup + normalization produce correct standings?** E.g. if team resolution mistakenly merges two clubs, standings get polluted. The champion tests will catch that. Also 2023 Série A comes ONLY from BR-Football — teams stateless (\"Sao Paulo\", \"Flamengo\", \"Gremio\", \"Atletico Mineiro\", \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Avai\", \"Bragantino\"...) — resolution quality decides 2023 table. \"Bragantino\" stateless → merges into (\"bragantino\", SP) ✓. \"Cuiaba\" → (\"cuiaba\", MT) ✓. \"Coritiba\" ✓. \"America MG\" ✓.\n\nLet me write the code now. Directory layout:\n\n```\nsoccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py       — version + exports\n  normalize.py      — name normalization, TeamRegistry, resolution\n  models.py         — Match, Player, TeamStatsRow etc.\n  loaders.py        — CSV → objects, dedup, registry build\n  analytics.py      — records, h2h, standings, biggest wins, competition stats, derbies\n  service.py        — SoccerDataService (formatted answers)\nserver.py           — MCP server (tools) + main\ntests/\n  conftest.py       — service fixture (session-scoped)\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_loaders.py\n  test_match_queries.py\n  test_team_queries.py\n  test_player_queries.py\n  test_competition_queries.py\n  test_statistics.py\n  test_sample_questions.py   — the 20+ BDD scenarios\n  test_server.py             — MCP protocol tests (list_tools, call_tool, InMemoryTransport e2e)\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml     — pytest config + project metadata\n```\n\nNote: \"Write all code files to the current directory\" ✓.\n\nBDD style per spec: I'll write tests with Gherkin-flavored docstrings:\n```python\ndef test_find_matches_between_two_teams():\n    \"\"\"Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n    Then I should receive a list of matches\n    And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n    \"\"\"\n```\nThat maps directly to the spec's Gherkin scenarios. \n\nNow writing the code. Start with normalize.py.\n\nCareful bits:\n- `strip_accents` with NFKD.\n- suffix regex: `(?:\\s*[-–]\\s*|\\s+)([A-Za-z]{2,3})$` — then check group in STATES (len 2) or COUNTRIES (len 3). Only strip if matches. But careful with names like \"Fortaleza EC\" — \"EC\" is 2 letters not a state ✓ kept. \"Sport Boys\" — \"Boys\" 4 ✓. \"CSA\"?? \"CSA\" is 3 uppercase letters — IS IT a country code? No, CSA not in COUNTRY_CODES ✓ safe. \"America FC Natal\" — \"Natal\" ✓ safe. What about \"... SC\"/\"... EC\"/\"... FC\" — safe (not states).\n  DANGER: teams ending with a 2-letter state code that ISN'T the state — e.g. hypothetical \"Time AP\"... \"Santos - AP\" is real (Santos-AP). Fine, data-driven.\n  Also \"4 de Julho - PI\" ✓. \"Ji-paraná - RO\" ✓.\n  Hmm — what about \"CA Parana\"? no suffix ✓.\n  What about names ending \"-GO\" etc. that are part of the name? e.g. \"Atlético-GO\" IS the state ✓ intended.\n- Paren removal: `re.sub(r'\\s*\\(([A-Za-z]{2,3})\\)\\s*$', ...)` → country in parens at END. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — content not 2-3 letters ✓ kept. But then base-alias \"america fc minas gerais\" — basic_norm must strip parens → \"america fc minas gerais\" ✓ (punct→space collapse).\n  What about mid-string \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\"? \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\": suffix strip \" - RJ\" ✓ → \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" → basic_norm → \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira\" → alias → (\"boavista\", RJ) ✓.\n- Country codes set: from Libertadores: EQU, PAR, URU, VEN, PER + likely ARG/CHI/COL/BOL/MEX. I'll include: ARG, BOL, CHI, COL, ECU, EQU, PAR, PER, URU, VEN, MEX, USA, CRC?, PAN? — keep to South American + Mexican: ARG, BOL, CHI, COL, EQU, PAR, PER, URU, VEN, MEX, GUA? just those.\n\n- TeamRegistry.resolve(query):\n  ```\n  q = collapse(query)\n  if q in BASE_ALIASES: base, region = BASE_ALIASES[q]  # full-name alias\n  else: base, region = parse(query)\n  # exact key\n  if (base, region) in entities → [it]\n  # alias on base alone\n  if base in BASE_ALIASES: base2, region2 = ...; if region is None or region==region2 → entity(base2, region2)\n  # base-only\n  cands = entities with base == final_base\n  if region: cands = [c for c in cands if c.region == region] or cands\n  if cands: return sorted by match_count\n  # substring: entities where q in base or q in raw variants (normalized)\n  substr = [e for e in entities if q and (q in e.base or any(q in collapse(v) for v in e.raw_names))]\n  if substr: return sorted by match_count\n  return []\n  ```\n  Query \"atletico mineiro\": parse → base \"atletico mineiro\" no region → not an entity base... check base alias: BASE_ALIASES[\"atletico mineiro\"] = (\"atletico\",\"MG\") → entity ✓.\n  Query \"atlético-mg\": collapse → \"atletico mg\" → hmm! parse first: suffix \"-MG\" → base \"atletico\" region MG → exact ✓. But wait — collapse(\"Atlético-MG\") = \"atletico mg\"; BASE_ALIASES lookup for \"atletico mg\"? I should apply parse BEFORE alias-lookup, and ALSO alias-lookup with the collapsed full string. Order: (1) full-string alias lookup on collapsed query → (\"atletico mineiro\" hits). (2) parse → (base, region). (3) base alias. (4) entity matching. \n  For \"atletico mg\": full-string alias? I could add \"atletico mg\" → (\"atletico\",\"MG\") but parse already handles. Skip.\n  Query \"palmeiras\": parse → (\"palmeiras\", None) → entities with base \"palmeiras\": [(\"palmeiras\", SP)] ✓ (since stateless merged).\n  Query \"palmeiras-sp\": parse → (\"palmeiras\", SP) ✓.\n  Query \"gremio\": (\"gremio\", RS) ✓.\n  Query \"saopaulo\" (no space): collapse(\"São Paulo\") = \"sao paulo\"; but user might type \"saopaulo\" → substring fallback: \"saopaulo\" in base \"sao paulo\"? NO. Hmm — also collapse with spaces removed for comparison? I'll add: also compare space-stripped forms. q2 = q.replace(\" \",\"\"); match if q2 in base.replace(\" \",\"\"). \"saopaulo\" in \"saopaulo\" ✓. Nice.\n  Query \"fla\"? → substring in \"flamengo\"? \"fla\" in \"flamengo\" ✓ → flamengo|RJ (also \"fluminense\"? \"fla\" not in it ✓). But careful short queries matching many things — acceptable; prominence sort.\n  Query \"corinthians\" ✓. Query \"coritiba pr\" → parse ✓. Query \"ceara\" → (\"ceara\", CE) ✓. Query \"sport club do recife\" → full alias ✓. Query \"recife\"? → no match — fine (or substring in raw \"Sport Club do Recife\" ✓ via raw-variant matching! q=\"recife\" in collapse(\"Sport Club do Recife\")=\"sport club do recife\" ✓ → sport|PE. Nice bonus.)\n  Query \"inter\" → substring in \"internacional\" ✓ both RS & SC → prominence RS first ✓.\n\n- BASE_ALIASES (applied to parsed base AND full-string): map base→(base,region):\n  ```\n  \"atletico mineiro\" → (\"atletico\",\"MG\")\n  \"clube atletico mineiro\" → (\"atletico\",\"MG\")\n  \"atletico paranaense\" → (\"atletico\",\"PR\")\n  \"athletico paranaense\" → (\"atletico\",\"PR\")\n  \"atletico goianiense\" → (\"atletico\",\"GO\")\n  \"athletico\" → (\"atletico\",\"PR\")\n  \"ca parana\" → (\"parana\",\"PR\")\n  \"sport recife\" → (\"sport\",\"PE\")\n  \"sport club do recife\" → (\"sport\",\"PE\")\n  \"vasco da gama\" → (\"vasco\",\"RJ\")\n  \"america fc natal\" → (\"america\",\"RN\")\n  \"america de natal\" → (\"america\",\"RN\")\n  \"america fc minas gerais\" → (\"america\",\"MG\")\n  \"vitoria ec\" → (\"vitoria\",\"BA\")\n  \"ec vitoria\" → (\"vitoria\",\"BA\")\n  \"ec bahia\" → (\"bahia\",\"BA\")\n  \"fortaleza ec\" → (\"fortaleza\",\"CE\")\n  \"fortaleza fc\" → (\"fortaleza\",\"CE\")\n  \"macae esporte\" → (\"macae\",\"RJ\")\n  \"portuguesa desportos\" → (\"portuguesa\",\"SP\")\n  \"nautico capibaribe\" → (\"nautico\",\"PE\")\n  \"gremio barueri\" → (\"barueri\",\"SP\")\n  \"gremio prudente\" → (\"barueri\",\"SP\")\n  \"red bull bragantino\" → (\"bragantino\",\"SP\")\n  \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira\" → (\"boavista\",\"RJ\")\n  \"operario ferroviario esporte c\" → (\"operario\",\"PR\")\n  \"esportivo bento goncalves\" → (\"esportivo\",\"RS\")\n  \"ceara sporting club\" → (\"ceara\",\"CE\")\n  \"santa cruz fc\" → (\"santa cruz\",\"PE\")\n  \"ec internacional\" → (\"internacional\",\"SC\")\n  \"fluminense de feira\" → hmm NOT an alias — it's its own base with state BA. parse handles \"Fluminense de Feira - BA\" → base \"fluminense de feira\" BA ✓. Stateless \"Fluminense De Feira\" → base same → stateless-merge → BA ✓. No alias needed.\n  \"santos fc\" → (\"santos\",\"SP\")? FIFA has \"Santos\" plain ✓ no need. Skip.\n  \"atletico cearense\"?? \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" → own entity ✓ no alias.\n  \"flamengo do piaui\" → own base + PI ✓ parse handles \"Flamengo do Piauí - PI\". Stateless variant? none. OK.\n  ```\n  Also full-string aliases (query-level): same dict works since I check full collapsed query first: \"atletico mineiro\" ✓, \"sport club do recife\" ✓, \"red bull bragantino\" ✓ etc.\n\n- Wait — one thing: \"Vasco da Gama RJ\" (BR-Football) → parse: suffix \" RJ\" → base \"vasco da gama\" region RJ → base-alias → (\"vasco\", \"RJ\") ✓. FIFA \"Vasco da Gama\" → base \"vasco da gama\" None → base-alias → (\"vasco\",\"RJ\") ✓. novo \"Vasco\" → (\"vasco\", None) → stateless-merge → RJ ✓. Brasileirao \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" → parse (\"vasco da gama\", RJ) → alias → (\"vasco\",\"RJ\") ✓. \n\n- Stateless-merge detail: entity with region None whose base has stateside entities → reassign to most prominent stateside. If base has NO stateside → stays None. E.g. \"boca juniors\" (None) stays.\n\n- Entity display names: curated DISPLAY map keyed by (base,region):\n  ```\n  (\"flamengo\",\"RJ\"): \"Flamengo\"\n  (\"fluminense\",\"RJ\"): \"Fluminense\"\n  (\"botafogo\",\"RJ\"): \"Botafogo\"\n  (\"vasco\",\"RJ\"): \"Vasco da Gama\"\n  (\"sao paulo\",\"SP\"): \"São Paulo\"\n  (\"corinthians\",\"SP\"): \"Corinthians\"\n  (\"palmeiras\",\"SP\"): \"Palmeiras\"\n  (\"santos\",\"SP\"): \"Santos\"\n  (\"gremio\",\"RS\"): \"Grêmio\"\n  (\"internacional\",\"RS\"): \"Internacional\"\n  (\"cruzeiro\",\"MG\"): \"Cruzeiro\"\n  (\"atletico\",\"MG\"): \"Atlético-MG\"\n  (\"atletico\",\"PR\"): \"Athletico-PR\"\n  (\"atletico\",\"GO\"): \"Atlético-GO\"\n  (\"bahia\",\"BA\"): \"Bahia\"\n  (\"vitoria\",\"BA\"): \"Vitória\"\n  (\"sport\",\"PE\"): \"Sport\"\n  (\"nautico\",\"PE\"): \"Náutico\"\n  (\"santa cruz\",\"PE\"): \"Santa Cruz\"\n  (\"ceara\",\"CE\"): \"Ceará\"\n  (\"fortaleza\",\"CE\"): \"Fortaleza\"\n  (\"america\",\"MG\"): \"América-MG\"\n  (\"america\",\"RN\"): \"América-RN\"\n  (\"chapecoense\",\"SC\"): \"Chapecoense\"\n  (\"avai\",\"SC\"): \"Avaí\"\n  (\"figueirense\",\"SC\"): \"Figueirense\"\n  (\"criciuma\",\"SC\"): \"Criciúma\"\n  (\"coritiba\",\"PR\"): \"Coritiba\"\n  (\"parana\",\"PR\"): \"Paraná\"\n  (\"goias\",\"GO\"): \"Goiás\"\n  (\"cuiaba\",\"MT\"): \"Cuiabá\"\n  (\"juventude\",\"RS\"): \"Juventude\"\n  (\"portuguesa\",\"SP\"): \"Portuguesa\"\n  (\"ponte preta\",\"SP\"): \"Ponte Preta\"\n  (\"bragantino\",\"SP\"): \"Red Bull Bragantino\"\n  (\"csa\",\"AL\"): \"CSA\"\n  (\"barueri\",\"SP\"): \"Grêmio Barueri/Prudente\"?? hmm — display \"Grêmio Prudente\"\n  (\"botafogo\",\"PB\"): \"Botafogo-PB\"\n  (\"botafogo\",\"SP\"): \"Botafogo-SP\"\n  (\"bahia de feira\",\"BA\"): \"Bahia de Feira\"\n  ...\n  ```\n  Fallback: base title-cased + region suffix if region: \"Atletico (MG)\"? For unambiguous-base entities WITHOUT region: capitalize base. For ambiguous bases: \"Base-REGION\". I'll implement: display = curated or (title(base) + (f\"-{region}\" if region and base_ambiguous else \"\")).\n\nHmm wait — for international teams like \"boca juniors\" (None): title(base) → \"Boca Juniors\" ✓. \"river plate uru\"? (\"river plate\", URU) vs (\"river plate\", None)? Both exist? \"River Plate-URU\" and Argentine \"River Plate\" stateless (from Libertadores). Display: \"River Plate (URU)\" vs \"River Plate\". My rule: region shown when base ambiguous → \"River Plate-URU\" and \"River Plate\" ✓ nice.\n\n- `Team` dataclass: key, base, region, display, raw_names (set), match_count, player_count.\n\nRegistry build inputs: raw name → count from match files. Then FIFA clubs: try resolve each club; if resolved to entity → player_count += n; else create... no — FIFA clubs are global; only count players for entities that resolve. Non-Brazilian clubs not needed as entities. But for search_players club filtering I match raw club strings directly (accent-insensitive contains), so no need for entities.\n\nWait — cross-file: team_profile(team=\"Grêmio\") shows \"Squad (FIFA data): 20 players, avg rating X\" ✓ via player_count + list. \n\nNow **loaders.py**:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    competition: str\n    season: int | None\n    date: dt.date | None\n    home: str  # entity key\n    away: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    round_label: str | None      # \"Round 22\", \"Semifinal\", \"Final\", \"Group Stage\", \"Quarterfinals\"\n    stage: str | None            # raw stage for Libertadores\n    venue: str | None\n    kickoff: str | None\n    halftime: str | None         # \"WON\"/\"DRAW\"/\"LOST\" from home perspective → format \"2-1\"? keep raw label\n    stats: MatchStats | None     # corners/shots/attacks\n    sources: list[str]\n```\n\nLoading functions per file returning list of `RawMatch` (with raw team names), then global assembly.\n\nParse dates: try formats. For \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → datetime → date. Time kept for kickoff when available.\n\nCup round label: {7: \"Semifinal\", 8: \"Final\"} else f\"Round {n}\".\nLibertadores stage label: map {\"group stage\": \"Group Stage\", \"round of 16\": \"Round of 16\", \"quarterfinals\": \"Quarterfinals\", \"semifinals\": \"Semifinals\", \"final\": \"Final\"}.\nBrasileirao: f\"Round {round}\".\nnovo: f\"Round {Rodada}\".\n\nDedup: dict[(competition, season, home_key, away_key)] → Match. On dup: extend sources; fill None fields (goals?? careful — fill goals only if kept has None AND dup has values: YES this fixes 2022 gap ✓; date/venue/round/stats likewise).\n\nWait — is filling goals from a dup safe? The dup is the same fixture from another file. If kept has None goals and dup has 3-1 → fill ✓. If kept has 1-0 and dup 3-1 (conflict) → keep first (load order priority). OK.\n\nBut hmm — one more dedup subtlety: what if the SAME (competition, season, home, away) appears twice within one file legitimately? For cups... I argued no. For leagues no. OK.\n\nActually — WAIT. There's one more case: Copa do Brasil teams CAN meet twice with same venue order?? Round 2: 2 legs (home team first leg away second). Actually the second leg swaps. If a lower-ranked team hosts both legs?? No — always swap. OK. But hmm, Libertadores group stage: team A hosts B once. Knockout: swap. ✓ Safe.\n\nBUT — Brasileirão + novo + BR-Football Serie A all feed \"Brasileirão Série A\". novo also includes... only Serie A ✓ verified.\n\nAnother subtlety: novo 2003-2019 & Brasileirao 2012-2022 & BRF 2014-2023 → merged Serie A seasons: 2003-2023 ✓ (with 2012-2019 from multiple sources).\n\n**Player loading**: \n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Player:\n    id: str; name: str; age: int|None; nationality: str; overall: int|None; potential: int|None\n    club: str  # raw club name\n    position: str|None; position_group: str|None; jersey: int|None\n    height: str|None; weight: str|None; foot: str|None; value: str|None; wage: str|None\n    skills: dict[str, int]\n```\nParse ints defensively. Skills: the named rating columns (Crossing...GKReflexes) — store dict of non-empty ones.\n\n**analytics.py**:\n\n```python\ndef team_record(matches, key, venue=None) -> Record(matches, wins, draws, losses, gf, ga)\ndef head_to_head(matches, k1, k2) -> H2H(matches, wins1, wins2, draws, gf1, gf2)\ndef standings(matches) -> list[Row(team, played, w, d, l, gf, ga, gd, pts)] sorted pts desc, then wins, gd, gf\ndef biggest_wins(matches, n) sorted by margin\ndef competition_stats(matches) -> Stats(total, avg_goals, home_wins, away_wins, draws, pct...)\ndef seasons_for(matches) ...\n```\n\n**service.py**: SoccerDataService(registry, matches, players) with formatted methods. Also helper `_resolve(team)` → (entity or None, note).\n\nNumber formatting: percentages 1 decimal; avg goals 2 decimals.\n\n**server.py**:\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nserver = MCPServer(name=\"brazilian-soccer\", instructions=..., description=...)\n\n@server.tool()\ndef search_matches(team: str | None = None, ...) -> str:\n    \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n    return get_service().search_matches(...)\n\ndef build_server() -> MCPServer: ...  # for tests\ndef main(): server.run()  # stdio\nif __name__ == \"__main__\": main()\n```\n\nHmm — with decorators at module level, tools get registered at import; data loads lazily on first tool call (get_service()). For tests, build_server() returns a fresh MCPServer with tools registered (decorator style inside function). Let me structure: `_register_tools(server)` function; `build_server()` creates + registers; module-level `server = build_server()` guarded... Actually for `python server.py` execution: `if __name__ == \"__main__\": build_server().run()`. And a module-level singleton for uvicorn-style? Not needed. Keep it simple: build_server() factory; main uses it.\n\nWait — MCP servers are usually started via `mcp dev server.py` / `claude mcp add -- python server.py`. The convention: module-level `mcp`/`server` variable OR main guard. I'll do both: module-level `server = build_server()` and `if __name__ == \"__main__\": server.run()`.\n\nTool param typing: mcp validates via pydantic; use Optional with defaults. Date params as strings \"YYYY-MM-DD\" (simpler than date objects for schema) — parse inside service.\n\n**Testing the server**: \n```python\n@pytest.mark.anyio\nasync def test_list_tools():\n    s = build_server()\n    tools = await s.list_tools()\n    names = [t.name for t in tools.tools]?  # check result shape — list_tools returns list[Tool] in 2.x? verify.\n    ...\nres = await s.call_tool(\"standings\", {\"competition\": \"brasileirao serie a\", \"season\": 2019})\n# res.content[0].text\n```\nNeed to check list_tools/call_tool return shapes. call_tool returns CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult — has .content list of content blocks, .isError maybe. Let me introspect in a scratch run while writing tests.\n\nanyio pytest plugin: anyio installed ships pytest plugin. Need `anyio_backend` fixture. I'll add in conftest:\n```python\n@pytest.fixture\ndef anyio_backend():\n    return \"asyncio\"\n```\n\nInMemoryTransport e2e:\n```python\nasync with InMemoryTransport(server) as streams:\n    async with ClientSession(streams[0], streams[1]) as session:  # TransportStreams is a tuple?\n        await session.initialize()\n        tools = await session.list_tools()\n        res = await session.call_tool(\"search_players\", {\"nationality\": \"Brazil\", \"limit\": 3})\n```\nCheck TransportStreams type — `from mcp.client._transport import TransportStreams` — probably NamedTuple(read_stream, write_stream). I'll check at test time.\n\nAlso — **data loading performance in tests**: 6 CSVs (~25MB) parse + registry ≈ 1-3s, session-scoped fixture ✓. Server tests call tools → same shared service singleton (module-level cache) ✓.\n\nNow, sample questions test (20+):\n1. When did Flamengo last play Corinthians? → search_matches(team=\"Flamengo\", opponent=\"Corinthians\", limit=...) → most recent date ✓\n2. What was the score? → same result includes score\n3. Who is Neymar (Jr)? → player_profile(\"Neymar Jr\")\n4. Which players play for Grêmio? → search_players(club=\"Grêmio\")\n5. Show me all derbies in 2019 → derbies(season=2019)\n6. What competitions has Palmeiras played in? → team_profile(\"Palmeiras\")\n7. Show me all Flamengo vs Fluminense matches → search_matches(team=..., opponent=...)\n8. What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023? → search_matches(team=\"Palmeiras\", season=2023)\n9. Find all Copa do Brasil finals → finals(competition=\"Copa do Brasil\")\n10. What is Corinthians' home record in 2022? → team_stats(team=\"Corinthians\", season=2022, venue=\"home\")\n11. Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023? → standings(2023) includes GF leader... or stats; I'll make standings show top scorer team. Test via standings output or team_stats iteration. I'll add in `stats` tool: top-scoring teams? Simpler: test standings(2023) top GF. Actually let me add optional \"top scorers\" info in stats tool: top teams by goals. Hmm — keep spec-focused: \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" → I'll include in stats tool output \"Top scoring team\". \n12. Compare Palmeiras and Santos head-to-head → head_to_head(\"Palmeiras\", \"Santos\")\n13. Find all Brazilian players → search_players(nationality=\"Brazil\", limit=...) (827)\n14. Who are the highest-rated players at Grêmio? → top_players(club=\"Grêmio\")\n15. Show me all forwards from São Paulo → search_players(club=\"São Paulo\"...) — FIFA has no São Paulo! → search_players(position=\"FWD\", club=...)? No São Paulo club in FIFA data. Use \"Show me all Brazilian forwards\" → search_players(nationality=\"Brazil\", position=\"FWD\"). ✓\n16. Who won the 2019 Brasileirão? → standings(2019)\n17. Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket → hmm \"bracket\" — semifinals+final of 2018: search_matches(competition=\"Libertadores\", season=2018, stage=\"final\") + ... I'll implement finals tool; bracket = knockouts: search_matches(stage=...) Libertadores stages. Test: search_matches(competition=\"libertadores\", season=2018, stage=\"final\") returns Boca vs River.\n18. Which teams were relegated in 2020? → standings(2020) relegated section\n19. What's the average goals per match in the Brasileirão? → stats(competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\")\n20. Which team has the best away record? → hmm — need a tool: team_stats with venue=\"away\" across all? I'll add to stats tool: \"best home/away records\" when competition-level & no team. Let me add tool `best_records(competition, season, venue)`? Or fold into stats output: top-3 home and away teams by win rate. I'll fold into stats tool.\n21. Show me the biggest wins in the dataset → biggest_wins()\n22. When did Flamengo last play? → search_matches(team=\"Flamengo\", limit=1)? I'll make search_matches sort desc by date by default (most recent first). Spec example lists 2023-09-03 first then 2023-05-28 — yes newest first ✓.\n23. Who is the highest-rated Brazilian player? → top_players(nationality=\"Brazil\", limit=1) → Neymar Jr 94? (FIFA 19: Neymar 92, Messi 94... spec says Neymar 92.) ✓\n24. What do you know about Atlético-MG? → team_profile\n\nThat's 20+. ✓\n\n**Champion validations** — I'll write the standings test with the historical champion table and see which pass; drop failing (2005, 2022) with comments.\n\nNow — requirements.txt:\n```\nmcp>=2.1.0\npytest>=8\npytest-cov\n```\n(coverage/pytest already in venv.)\n\npyproject.toml — minimal: [project] name soccer-mcp, requires-python >=3.12, deps; [tool.pytest.ini_options] testpaths=[\"tests\"], anyio stuff? anyio plugin auto. Add `-ra` etc.\n\nAlright — also the **prompt** file `prompts.txt` — let me peek at it quickly (it might contain benchmark prompts to satisfy). Let me read it before finalizing.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0590b0d87001ru6OjOY9F87zqc","sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","messageID":"msg_0590855f40018oMA38