":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-d69f3f5e81d5/soccer_mcp/loaders.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-d69f3f5e81d5/soccer_mcp/loaders.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-d69f3f5e81d5/soccer_mcp/loaders.py\n@@ -445,14 +445,14 @@\n     seen[dedup_key] = match\n     by_pair.setdefault((row.competition, home_key, away_key), []).append(match)\n     matches.append(match)\n \n-# Sort: competition, season, date (None dates last within group).\n+# Sort: competition, season, date (matches without dates last within group).\n matches.sort(\n     key=lambda m: (\n         m.competition,\n         m.season if m.season is not None else 0,\n-        m.date is not None,\n+        m.date is None,\n         m.date,\n         m.away,\n     )\n )\n","additions":2,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"soccer_mcp/loaders.py","time":{"start":1788200545955,"end":1788200545958}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's a bug in the sort key: `m.date is not None` sorts False (0) before True (1), meaning None dates FIRST. I want None dates LAST → sort key should be `(m.date is None, m.date)` — hmm, `m.date is not None` ascending puts False (None dates) first. Fix: use `m.date is None` (False=0 for real dates first... wait I want real dates first, None last → key ascending: real (False=0) first ✓ with `m.date is None`. But then `m.date` itself: comparing None with None among trailing group — equal keys fine; but tuple comparison compares element-wise: for two None-date matches, both have (True, None)... comparing None < None fails? No — if first elements equal (True==True), then compares m.date (None vs None) — `None < None` raises TypeError! Actually no: sorted uses `__lt__` on tuples; when first elements are equal it compares second elements None vs None → TypeError: '<' not supported between instances of 'NoneType' and 'NoneType'. Hmm wait — only if it needs to compare them; tuple comparison does `(a[0] == b[0]) and (a[1] < b[1])`... Actually tuple `<` compares element-wise: finds first UNEQUAL position and compares. None == None is True (equal), so it moves to next element m.away. Equal elements don't trigger `<`. But wait, `None == None` → True → skip to next ✓. So (True, None, away1) vs (True, None, away2) → compares away strings ✓. And mixed (False, date, ...) vs (True, None, ...) → False < True → date-match first ✓. But what about two matches with real dates: (False, date1, ...) vs (False, date2, ...) → date1 < date2 ✓ fine.\n\nHmm wait, current code has `m.date is not None` → real dates get True=1 → AFTER None(False=0). That's wrong order (I want newest... no wait, ascending date within season — oldest first, None last). Current: None dates FIRST. Fix to `m.date is None`.\n\nThen service will reverse for \"most recent first\" display. OK.\n\nAlso the season sort: `m.season if m.season is not None else 0` — None-season matches sort at season 0 → first within competition. Minor; acceptable.\n\nLet me fix the sort key.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0590eee90001Nx2Ca36mmXxJUp","sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","messageID":"msg_0590eab1b001TTx5rTlrjw8PFB"}}
