mpetition == \"Brasileirão Série A\"\n+            and (r.get(\"date\") or \"\").startswith(\"2016-01-30\")\n+            and (r.get(\"home\") or \"\").strip() == \"Brasilia FC\"\n+        ):\n+            continue\n+        when, _ = parse_date(r.get(\"date\", \"\"))\n         ht = (r.get(\"ht_result\") or \"\").strip().upper() or None\n         stats = MatchStats(\n             home_corners=parse_int(r.get(\"home_corner\", \"\")),\n             away_corners=parse_int(r.get(\"away_corner\", \"\")),\n","additions":10,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"soccer_mcp/loaders.py","time":{"start":1788200973567,"end":1788200973569}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The BR-Football file has a mislabeled row: \"Serie A, Brasilia FC vs CA Taguatinga, 2016-01-30\" — these are Brasília-DF clubs, this is a Copa Verde match (or similar) mislabeled as Serie A in the source. One bad row. It slightly pollutes 2016 Serie A (adds 1 draw to two phantom \"teams\" that aren't in Serie A). It doesn't affect the champion, but pollutes list_teams for 2016 Serie A (22 teams incl. 2 phantoms with 1 match each).\n\nShould I filter it? The data-driven approach: keep it (it's in the source) — but standings will show two extra teams at the bottom with 1 match each. It'd be misleading for \"which teams were relegated in 2016\" (phantoms at bottom). Hmm — relegation list = bottom 4 → would include phantoms.\n\nOptions:\n(a) Leave as-is (data-driven, document caveat).\n(b) Heuristic filter: in standings, that's hard to generalize.\n(c) Special-case: drop this known-bad row in the loader with a comment.\n\nThe spec asks \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" style queries — correctness matters for demo quality. A single known-bad row: I'll filter rows where tournament='Serie A' and both teams are... too specific. Cleaner: drop Serie A/B/C rows from BR-Football where a team has only 1-2 matches in that competition+season? Over-engineering.\n\nPragmatic: hard-drop this specific malformed row in the BR-Football loader with a clear comment (documented data-quality fix). It's a demo server; documented curation is fine. Actually — let me make it slightly general: drop BR-Football league rows where BOTH teams are not present in any other file's league data for that season... too complex. I'll special-case Brasilia FC vs CA Taguatinga 2016 in Serie A. Hmm, actually a cleaner general rule: for league competitions, drop matches where either team plays fewer than 3 league matches in that season within the same source (real league teams play 38). But computing that requires cross-row analysis of the raw source — doable but adds complexity for ONE bad row.\n\nDecision: simple documented skip of this one fixture. I'll add a `_KNOWN_BAD_ROWS` check keyed on (tournament, date, home, away).\n\nNow — standings tool: relegated = bottom N. For 2023, missing 3 matches; the tool will show match counts so it's transparent. Good.\n\nNext: write service.py — the formatted query layer. This is the biggest file. Let me design its API:\n\n```python\nclass SoccerDataService:\n    def __init__(self, kb): registry, matches, players; precompute indexes:\n        - matches_by_competition: dict[str, list[Match]]\n        - competitions: dict[str, set[int]] seasons\n    helpers:\n        _parse_season(value) -> int|None  (accept 2019, \"2019\")\n        _parse_date_str -> date|None\n        _filter_matches(competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, team_key=None, opponent_key=None, stage=None, venue=None)\n        _resolve_one(team) -> (Team, note) or raises ValueError-ish → return None + message\n        _format_match(m) -> \"2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Série A 2023, Round 22)\"\n    public methods (return str):\n        list_competitions()\n        list_teams(competition=None, season=None, limit=50)\n        search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=20)\n        head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)\n        team_stats(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=\"all\")\n        team_profile(team)\n        standings(competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\", season=None, limit=None)\n        finals(competition=None, season=None)\n        search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)\n        player_profile(name)\n        top_players(club=None, nationality=None, position=None, limit=10)\n        biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)\n        stats(competition=None, season=None)\n        derbies(season=None, competition=None)\n```\n\nCompetition resolution: accept \"brasileirao\", \"Serie A\", \"copa do brasil\", \"libertadores\" etc. — use COMPETITIONS dict + accent-insensitive match on canonical names. Also allow \"série b\"/\"serie b\".\n\nPlayer filtering: nationality case-insensitive (\"Brazil\" matches \"Brazil\"; also \"brasil\"→\"Brazil\"? I'll normalize: accept \"brazil\"/\"brasil\"→\"Brazil\"). club: substring accent-insensitive on club name. position: accept codes (\"ST\") or groups (\"FWD\", \"forward\", \"atacante\"?). Keep English: GK/DEF/MID/FWD or full \"goalkeeper\",\"defender\",\"midfielder\",\"forward\".\n\nOutput formats following spec examples. Also every tool returns helpful \"not found\" messages with suggestions.\n\nNumber formatting helpers.\n\nLet me now write service.py carefully. Also team_stats: \"Corinthians home record (2022 Brasileirão)\" format from spec:\n```\nCorinthians home record (2022 Brasileirão):\n- Matches: 19\n- Wins: 11, Draws: 5, Losses: 3\n- Goals For: 28, Goals Against: 15\n- Win rate: 57.9%\n```\nMy TeamRecord.line() produces multiline similar ✓.\n\nsearch_matches output:\n```\nMatches matching 'Flamengo vs Fluminense' (Brasileirão Série A) — 17 matches:\n1. 2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Série A, Round 22)\n...\n(showing 20 of 37)\n```\nMost recent first (spec example shows newest first ✓).\n\nstandings output:\n```\n2019 Brasileirão Série A — Final Standings (calculated from 380 matches):\n1. Flamengo - 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L) - Champion\n2. Santos - 74 pts ...\n...\nRelegated (bottom 4): ...\n```\nThe spec's \"Champion\" tag on row 1 ✓.\n\nFor cups: standings() on Copa do Brasil → informative message pointing to finals() + stats.\n\nteam_profile:\n```\nFlamengo (RJ) — Team Profile\nCompetitions played (in dataset): Brasileirão Série A (2012-2022), Copa do Brasil (...), Copa Libertadores (...)\nAll-time record (all competitions, scored matches): 580 matches — 300W 150D 130L, goals 950:700\nTitles in dataset: Brasileirão Série A 2019, 2020\nBiggest win: ...\nSquad (FIFA data): 20 players, avg rating 74. Top: Everton Ribeiro (82)...\n```\n\nfinals():\n```\nCopa do Brasil finals in dataset:\n2013: Flamengo beat Atlético-PR (2-0 on aggregate: 1-1, 2-0)\n```\nHmm — computing aggregate winners from 2 legs: sum goals. For Libertadores single finals: winner direct. For the NA row (2022 Flamengo vs Athletico, no score): show as \"score not recorded in dataset\". I'll present each final's legs and aggregate winner when computable.\n\nstats():\n```\nBrasileirão Série A — all seasons (8,403 matches):\n- Matches with scores: 8,283\n- Average goals per match: 2.46\n- Home wins: 47.2%, Draws: 27.5%, Away wins: 25.3%\n- Biggest win: X\n- Best home record: ... (win rate, min 50 matches)\n- Best away record: ...\n```\nWait \"min_matches=10\" in best_records — for all-seasons aggregate, teams like Flamengo have 800 matches; min 50 filters small clubs. For single season, min 10 might drop... fine, use min_matches parameter by context: if season given → min 5 else 50. Hmm, keep simple: min_matches = 10 if season else 50. Also top-scoring team info for \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" — I'll include \"Top scorers\" (most goals for) in stats output when season specified... actually standings already