 2-0). Are these the same match or different? Copa do Brasil 2019: Aparecidense played Ponte Preta in round 1 (April, single leg). The Feb 12 match... Aparecidense vs Ponte Preta in February 2019 — that could be Copa Verde or a friendly mislabeled in BR-Football, OR a different competition. They're 50 days apart with different scores — genuinely different rows. Since Copa do Brasil is single-elimination, they can't both be Copa do Brasil matches vs each other... but I can't reliably determine which is wrong. This is ONE edge row — acceptable to leave (data-driven).\n\nSo Copa do Brasil dedup is actually clean: 1,697 unique matches with only 1 ambiguous pair. My earlier estimate of ~900 overlap was wrong — fine.\n\nNow let me also double-check Série B and Série C don't have issues:\n- Série B 3,655: BR-Football Serie B 3,677 raw - some dedup?? Only one file has Serie B — how did 3,677 become 3,655? 22 fewer! Same-file dedups — league key (season, home, away) within BR-Football Serie B — 22 rows with duplicate (season, home, away) — data errors in source (e.g. the Goias-Corinthians postponed match recorded at both dates!) OR... hmm wait, postponed: recorded twice with different dates → my dedup keeps FIRST (earlier loaded = earlier in file order... rows in file order). If the file has both original and actual dates, we keep whichever comes first in the file. Meh — for stats, ONE instance counted — better than double-counting.\n\nBut WAIT — actually I should double check something important: is it possible that within BR-Football, a postponed league match appears TWICE (original date + played date)? If so, dedup keeps one ✓ good. And 22 such rows exist. Fine.\n\n- Série C 1,749: raw 1,807 → 58 deduped within-file. Same story.\n\nSérie A 8,402: 4,180 + 6,886 + 3,689 = 14,755 raw → 8,402 unique. Sanity: 21 seasons × 380 + 2×552 + 462 = 7,980 + 1,104 + 462 = 9,546?? Hmm wait: 2003-2004: 552 each; 2005: 462; 2006-2023: 380 × 18 = 6,840. Total = 552+552+462+6,840 = 8,406. I have 8,402 — 4 short (2009: -1 known novo gap, 2023: -3 known BRF gap). ✓✓ PERFECT accounting.\n\nSérie B: expected? 2014-2023, 20 teams → 380/season × 10 = 3,800 expected; have 3,655 (BRF source incomplete for some seasons, e.g. 2014 28 Paraná matches...). Source gaps, fine.\n\nNow the numbers are solid. Time to write the tests. Let me update the todo list and write:\n1. tests/conftest.py — session fixture\n2. tests/test_normalize.py\n3. tests/test_loaders.py\n4. tests/test_match_queries.py (BDD GWT)\n5. tests/test_team_queries.py\n6. tests/test_player_queries.py\n7. tests/test_competition_queries.py\n8. tests/test_statistics.py\n9. tests/test_sample_questions.py — 20+ questions\n10. tests/test_server.py — MCP protocol tests incl. InMemoryTransport e2e\n\nThen requirements.txt, pyproject.toml, README.\n\nFirst, check anyio pytest plugin availability for async tests.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0591c1e31001xZjMTRvNAewVig","sessionID":"ses_fa7021914ffexcBCZnAM1HV8iX","messageID":"msg_0591bc75d001rc4aQlXMC5XFZU"}}
